Track 2: Sister Zola’s Seven-Second Trench-Hop
- Story & Context: Confined for twenty-eight days of decompression inside Habitat Chamber Four off Bermuda, Sister Zola watched the salt crystals bloom along the viewport seams until cabin fever turned into rhythmic delirium. To prevent her hands from shaking before her saturation welding shifts, she invented an agonizing hopscotch game over the pressurized oxygen pipes, singing syncopated dancehall chants into her rebreather while striking the brass manifold with an iron mallet. Her confession to the diving physician was entered into the medical log after she refused sedation, insisting the undersea pressure had tuned her ribs into vibraphone tines: "My blood run high-test, dem cannot quench di heat!" The track was recorded entirely inside that steel cylinder, capturing the metallic clang of her work boots striking the pressurized steel deck.
